dcdiag: Runs a general Domain Controller diagnostic. Checks common AD/DNS/replication and DC health issues.

dcdiag /v: Runs dcdiag in verbose mode, giving much more detailed diagnostic information. Useful when troubleshooting a problem.

dcdiag /test:dns: Specifically tests DNS health on the Domain Controller. Checks DNS configuration, DNS registration, and AD-related DNS records.

dcdiag /test:replications: Tests Active Directory replication between Domain Controllers and looks for replication-related problems.

repadmin /replsummary: Gives a summary of AD replication health across Domain Controllers. Helps identify Domain Controllers with replication failures or errors.

repadmin /showrepl: Shows detailed inbound replication status for the Domain Controller, including which Domain Controllers it replicates from and whether replication succeeded or failed.

net share: Displays the shared folders and resources currently shared by the server.

net share SYSVOL: Checks and displays the SYSVOL share. SYSVOL contains important domain files such as Group Policy and logon-related files.

net share NETLOGON: Checks and displays the NETLOGON share. NETLOGON is used by Domain Controllers for logon scripts and other domain authentication-related files.


# Real World Use:

dcdiag: Runs a general DC health check (AD, DNS, services, connectivity). Real-world use: Your first line of defense when users suddenly can't log in or Group Policy fails to apply.

dcdiag /v: Runs the diagnostic in verbose mode for deeper test results and warnings. Real-world use: Used when a standard dcdiag reports a failure but doesn't give you enough detail to find the root cause.

 dcdiag /test:dns: Specifically checks DNS configurations, SRV registrations, and zone records. Real-world use: Crucial when workstations are failing to find a Domain Controller on the network.

 dcdiag /test:replications: Targets AD database replication health between DCs. Real-world use: Used when changes (like a new user or password update) made on one DC aren't showing up on another.

repadmin /replsummary: Provides a high-level summary of replication health across all DCs. Real-world use: A quick way to spot exactly which Domain Controllers are failing replication without checking each one individually.

repadmin /showrepl: Displays detailed inbound replication data, partners, and error codes. Real-world use: Once replsummary flags an error, you run this to find out exactly which replication partner is failing and when it last worked.

net share: Lists all shared resources on the server. Real-world use: Run immediately after a DC reboot to quickly confirm that the server is actually sharing out its critical identity folders.

 net share SYSVOL: Verifies the local policy share exists. This holds GPOs, templates, and scripts (replicated via modern DFSR). Real-world use: If users aren't receiving updated Group Policies, you run this to confirm the share is alive before diving into background DFSR troubleshooting.

net share NETLOGON: Verifies the log on share exists (maps to SYSVOL\domain\scripts for legacy scripts). Real-world use: Its presence is a vital indicator that the DC is ready to handle authentication traffic, though overall health must still be verified via dcdiag.

Use net share for quick share availability, repadmin for database sync issues, and dcdiag for comprehensive structural health.

Summary:
dcdiag → Is my DC healthy?
dcdiag /v → Give me detailed health information
dcdiag /test:dns → Is DNS working correctly?
dcdiag /test:replications → Is AD replication working?
repadmin /replsummary → Which DCs have replication problems?
repadmin /showrepl → Show me the detailed replication relationships/errors
net share → What shares exist?
SYSVOL → Is the SYSVOL share present?
NETLOGON → Is the NETLOGON share present?

dcdiag: Runs a general Domain Controller diagnostic. It checks the overall health of the Domain Controller, including Active Directory, DNS, replication, services, 
connectivity, and other important DC functions. In a real-world environment, it is commonly used as the first command when troubleshooting Domain Controller health issues. 
For example, if users suddenly cannot log in or Group Policy is not applying, an administrator can run dcdiag to look for common Domain Controller problems.

dcdiag /v: Runs the Domain Controller diagnostic in verbose mode and provides much more detailed information than the standard dcdiag command. It displays additional test
results, configuration details, errors, warnings, and information about individual tests. In a real-world environment, this is useful when the basic dcdiag output shows a
problem and more information is required to identify the root cause. For example, if dcdiag reports that a test has failed but does not provide enough information to 
understand why, dcdiag /v can provide additional details that help the administrator investigate the issue.

dcdiag /test:dns: Performs specific DNS-related health checks on the Domain Controller. It verifies DNS configuration, DNS registration, connectivity to DNS servers, and 
important Active Directory DNS records. DNS is very important to Active Directory because Domain Controllers use DNS to locate other Domain Controllers and services. In a 
real-world environment, DNS problems can cause users to have login problems, prevent computers from locating a Domain Controller, and cause Active Directory replication
problems. For example, if a workstation cannot find a Domain Controller when a user tries to log in, an administrator can run dcdiag /test:dns to check whether DNS is 
correctly configured.

dcdiag /test:replications: Performs a specific test of Active Directory replication health. It checks whether replication between Domain Controllers is functioning correctly
and identifies replication-related failures or errors. Active Directory information must replicate between Domain Controllers so that changes made on one Domain Controller 
are available on the others. In a real-world environment, replication problems can result in different Domain Controllers having different user accounts, passwords, 
Group Policy information, or other Active Directory data. For example, if a new user is created on one Domain Controller but cannot be found when another Domain Controller 
is checked, replication should be investigated.

repadmin /replsummary: Provides a summary of Active Directory replication health across the Domain Controllers in the environment. It shows replication failures, error counts
, and information about Domain Controllers experiencing replication problems. In a real-world environment, this command provides a quick way to identify which Domain Controllers 
may have replication issues without reviewing every individual replication connection. For example, in an environment with several Domain Controllers, an administrator can run 
repadmin /replsummary to quickly see whether replication is healthy across the domain and identify any Domain Controllers reporting failures.

repadmin /showrepl: Displays detailed inbound Active Directory replication information for a Domain Controller. It shows the replication partners, naming contexts being 
replicated, the last successful replication, and any replication errors. In a real-world environment, this command is useful for investigating exactly which Domain Controller
or replication connection is failing. For example, if repadmin /replsummary reports a replication error, an administrator can use repadmin /showrepl to determine which 
replication partner is failing and when the last successful replication occurred.

net share: Displays all shared folders and resources currently being shared by the server. In a real-world Domain Controller environment, this provides a quick way to confirm
that important domain-related shares, such as SYSVOL and NETLOGON, are available. These shares are important for normal domain operations because clients need access to
certain domain resources during authentication and Group Policy processing. For example, an administrator can run net share after a Domain Controller restart to confirm that
the expected SYSVOL and NETLOGON shares are present.

net share SYSVOL: Checks whether the SYSVOL share exists and is available on the Domain Controller. The presence of SYSVOL confirms that the share is being provided by the 
server, but it does not by itself prove that SYSVOL replication is healthy. SYSVOL contains important domain files, including Group Policy Objects (GPOs), Group Policy 
templates, startup and shutdown scripts, and other policy-related files. In modern Active Directory environments, SYSVOL replication is normally handled by DFSR, while 
older environments may use the legacy NTFRS technology. These files must be replicated and available across Domain Controllers so users and computers receive the correct 
policies regardless of which Domain Controller they use. In a real-world environment, a missing SYSVOL share can cause Group Policy processing and other domain functions
to fail. For example, if users are not receiving a new Group Policy setting, an administrator can check whether the SYSVOL share exists and then use appropriate DFSR and 
Active Directory replication checks to investigate further.

net share NETLOGON: Checks whether the NETLOGON share exists and is available on the Domain Controller. NETLOGON is an important share provided by Domain Controllers and is 
associated with domain authentication and logon operations. It maps to the SYSVOL\domain\scripts folder and can host legacy logon scripts that are used when users log on to the 
domain. The presence of the NETLOGON share is a useful indicator that the Domain Controller is providing this expected domain service, but it does not by itself prove that 
the entire Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) role is healthy or that the server is fully functional as a Domain Controller. Other commands, such as dcdiag and repadmin,
should be used to verify overall Domain Controller health and replication. In a real-world environment, a missing NETLOGON share can indicate a problem with the Domain Controller 
or related services. For example, if a user's logon script is not running or a recently promoted Domain Controller does not have the NETLOGON share, an administrator can check 
the share and then investigate the underlying AD DS, SYSVOL, or replication issue.


